2026-07-22T10:52:53.547982Z  INFO session_loop{thread_id=019f8937-f74a-7f13-8a67-a1d0cb33b562}:submission_dispatch{otel.name="op.dispatch.user_input" submission.id="019f8972-0506-7173-9c8c-676605ea2655" codex.op="user_input"}:turn{otel.name="session_task.turn" thread.id=019f8937-f74a-7f13-8a67-a1d0cb33b562 turn.id=019f8972-0506-7173-9c8c-676605ea2655 model=gpt-5.6-luna codex.turn.reasoning_effort=medium}: codex_core::stream_events_utils: ToolCall: exec const patch = "*** Begin Patch\n*** Add File: /Users/k1t94/Work/Projects/kaynann/knn-water-vending/specs/001-project-bootstrap/research.md\n+# Research: Project Bootstrap Foundation\n+\n+## Decision: pnpm 10 workspace\n+\n+- **Rationale**: One lockfile and root scripts can orchestrate independent `apps/api` and `apps/mobile` packages without coupling their runtime code. pnpm's workspace model fits a monorepo and supports filtered commands.\n+- **Alternatives considered**: npm workspaces (less focused filtering); Yarn (valid, but no existing repository convention and unnecessary migration cost).\n+- **Implementation note**: Pin the package manager through `packageManager` in the root manifest and commit `pnpm-lock.yaml`.\n+\n+## Decision: Node.js 24.x LTS\n+\n+- **Rationale**: Node's official release page lists v24 as LTS on 2026-07-22. It is the current LTS line suitable for the API and tooling.\n+- **Alternatives considered**: Node 22.x LTS (older supported line, fallback if a selected native dependency does not support 24); Node 26 Current (not selected because production guidance favors LTS).\n+- **Source**: https://nodejs.org/en/about/previous-releases\n+\n+## Decision: React Native CLI for `apps/mobile`\n+\n+- **Rationale**: The MVP requires native camera/QR, maps/location, deep links, push, safe storage, and platform-specific permission configuration. CLI keeps native project ownership explicit.\n+- **Alternatives considered**: Expo (officially recommended for many new apps and simpler native setup; rejected for this bootstrap because the project requires explicit native iOS/Android control). Revisit only with explicit scope approval.\n+- **Source**: https://reactnative.dev/docs/environment-setup\n+\n+## Decision: Prisma ORM with migration-first PostgreSQL workflow\n+\n+- **Rationale**: Prisma provides a typed schema/client workflow and a first-class migration path compatible with the TypeScript API. Migrations are committed and applied explicitly; automatic schema synchronization is prohibited.\n+- **Alternatives considered**: TypeORM (NestJS ecosystem fit, but less attractive generated type workflow); Drizzle (strong SQL control, but no existing repository evidence).\n+- **Source**: https://www.prisma.io/docs/getting-started\n+\n+## Decision: Vitest for API and mobile JavaScript tests\n+\n+- **Rationale**: One fast runner can cover API unit/integration smoke tests and mobile JavaScript bootstrap tests; native device tests remain a separate prerequisite.\n+- **Alternatives considered**: Jest (mature React Native default and fallback); Node built-in test runner (not sufficient for the mobile transform/module ecosystem).\n+- **Implementation note**: Select the React Native transformer/preset during dependency bootstrap and record it in the app manifest.\n+\n+## Decision: Docker Compose with PostgreSQL, Redis, and Mosquitto\n+\n+- **Rationale**: Compose provides a disposable local topology with healthchecks and named network endpoints. Mosquitto supplies a local MQTT broker for connectivity tests without pretending to be the vending-machine protocol.\n+- **Alternatives considered**: Testcontainers (more isolated but more moving parts for onboarding); in-process MQTT mock (does not validate broker connectivity and topic transport).\n+\n+## Resolved Unknowns\n+\n+- Package manager: pnpm 10.\n+- Node.js baseline: 24.x LTS.\n+- ORM: Prisma.\n+- Test runner: Vitest, with React Native-specific transform configuration as needed.\n+- Local MQTT emulator: Mosquitto broker with bootstrap-only topics/credentials; no dispensing payload contract.\n+- Mobile native build: React Native CLI with Xcode/Android SDK/JDK prerequisites documented separately from API checks.\n*** Add File: /Users/k1t94/Work/Projects/kaynann/knn-water-vending/specs/001-project-bootstrap/data-model.md\n+# Data Model: Project Bootstrap Foundation\n+\n+This feature does not introduce MVP business entities. The following technical entities exist only to define safe startup and validation boundaries.\n+\n+## Environment Contract\n+\n+- `NODE_ENV`: `development`, `test`, or `production`; validated against the allowed set.\n+- `API_PORT`: local HTTP port; validated as an integer in the configured range.\n+- `DATABASE_URL`: local PostgreSQL connection string; required by the API runtime and never returned in responses/logs.\n+- `REDIS_URL`: local Redis connection string; required by the API runtime and never returned in responses/logs.\n+- `MQTT_URL`: local broker URL; required for connectivity checks and never returned in responses/logs.\n+- `MQTT_CLIENT_ID`: non-secret local client identifier; validated as non-empty.\n+\n+Production credentials, provider keys, signing material, certificates, and webhook secrets are not part of this model.\n+\n+## Service Dependency Status\n+\n+- `name`: one of `postgres`, `redis`, `mqtt`.\n+- `status`: `up` or `down`.\n+- `latencyMs`: optional bounded integer for diagnostics.\n+- `errorCode`: optional stable non-secret diagnostic code.\n+\n+The response may include dependency names and statuses only. It must not include URLs, usernames, passwords, raw driver errors, or environment values.\n+\n+## Healthcheck Result\n+\n+- `status`: `ok` when all required dependencies are available; `degraded` or `failed` otherwise.\n+- `service`: API service identifier.\n+- `version`: application version or build identifier safe for exposure.\n+- `dependencies`: allow-listed `Service Dependency Status` entries.\n+\n+No user, session, payment, balance, machine-dispense, or referral records are created by this feature.\n*** Add File: /Users/k1t94/Work/Projects/kaynann/knn-water-vending/specs/001-project-bootstrap/contracts/healthcheck.md\n+# Healthcheck Contract\n+\n+## Endpoint\n+\n+`GET /health`\n+\n+## Success\n+\n+- HTTP `200`\n+- `status` is `ok`\n+- All required local dependencies are represented with `status: up`\n+\n+Example shape:\n+\n+```json\n+{\n+  \"status\": \"ok\",\n+  \"service\": \"kaynann-api\",\n+  \"version\": \"<safe-build-id>\",\n+  \"dependencies\": {\n+    \"postgres\": { \"status\": \"up\" },\n+    \"redis\": { \"status\": \"up\" },\n+    \"mqtt\": { \"status\": \"up\" }\n+  }\n+}\n+```\n+\n+## Failure\n+\n+- HTTP `503` when one or more required dependencies are unavailable.\n+- `status` is `degraded` or `failed`.\n+- Each unavailable dependency exposes only a stable `errorCode`, not a raw driver error or connection string.\n+\n+## Security Rules\n+\n+- Never return `DATABASE_URL`, `REDIS_URL`, `MQTT_URL`, credentials, tokens, private keys, stack traces, or raw connection errors.\n+- Keep the endpoint unauthenticated for local readiness checks, but expose no business or user data.\n+- Keep response fields explicitly shaped; do not serialize configuration or exception objects.\n*** Add File: /Users/k1t94/Work/Projects/kaynann/knn-water-vending/specs/001-project-bootstrap/quickstart.md\n+# Bootstrap Quickstart Validation\n+\n+This guide defines the validation flow for the implementation. Commands are the planned stable root scripts; after implementation they must match the actual root `package.json` and be copied into `specs/000-project-onboarding/repo-map.md`.\n+\n+## Prerequisites\n+\n+- Node.js 24.x LTS.\n+- pnpm 10.x enabled through Corepack or the documented equivalent.\n+- Docker and Docker Compose.\n+- For mobile native builds: Xcode and CocoaPods on macOS for iOS, or Android Studio, Android SDK, and JDK 17 for Android.\n+\n+## Setup\n+\n+```sh\n+pnpm install --frozen-lockfile\n+cp .env.example .env\n+pnpm services:up\n+pnpm db:migrate\n+```\n+\n+The copied `.env` must contain local placeholders only. No production or external-provider credentials are needed.\n+\n+## Static Validation\n+\n+```sh\n+pnpm lint\n+pnpm typecheck\n+pnpm test\n+pnpm build:api\n+pnpm build:mobile\n+```\n+\n+`build:mobile` requires the platform SDKs. When they are unavailable, run the mobile static checks and report the missing native prerequisite rather than substituting a different command.\n+\n+## Startup Verification\n+\n+```sh\n+pnpm services:up\n+pnpm verify:start\n+```\n+\n+Expected result: the script starts or verifies the local service stack, starts the API, calls `GET /health`, confirms HTTP `200` and all dependency statuses `up`, then exits `0`. It exits non-zero with a redacted diagnostic if a dependency or startup step fails.\n+\n+## Out Of Scope Check\n+\n+The quickstart must not require OTP, payments, wallet/balance operations, referral codes, physical dispense commands, external provider credentials, or a real vending machine.\n*** End Patch";
text(await tools.apply_patch(patch));
